{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KtQNxxdr6YhTtCJnr96BTa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# seq_detector\n2\t\n3\tA Moore finite-state machine that detects the serial bit pattern `1011` on `din`, with overlapping\n4\tmatches allowed. The state register must be **one-hot** encoded.\n5\t\n6\t## Ports\n7\t| Port  | Dir | Width | Description |\n8\t|-------|-----|-------|-------------|\n9\t| clk   | in  | 1     | Rising-edge clock |\n10\t| rst_n | in  | 1     | Asynchronous active-low reset |\n11\t| din   | in  | 1     | Serial input, sampled on each rising edge of `clk` |\n12\t| found | out | 1     | Registered detection flag |\n13\t\n14\t## Behavior\n15\t- One bit of `din` is sampled at every rising edge of `clk` (there is no enable).\n16\t- The FSM tracks the longest suffix of the bits sampled so far (since reset) that is a prefix of\n17\t  `1011`. States (one-hot): `IDLE` (no useful prefix), `S1` (\"1\"), `S10` (\"10\"), `S101` (\"101\"),\n18\t  `S1011` (full match).\n19\t- Matches overlap: the trailing bits of one match may begin the next. For example the input\n20\t  stream `1011011` produces two matches, and `10101011` produces a match at its last bit\n21\t  (the `101` inside `1010` must not be thrown away).\n22\t- `found` is a flip-flop. After the rising edge at which the final `1` of a `1011` pattern is\n23\t  sampled, `found` is 1 for that clock cycle (i.e. `found` is 1 exactly while the FSM is in\n24\t  `S1011`); otherwise it is 0.\n25\t- While `rst_n` is low the FSM is in `IDLE` and `found` is 0. Bits sampled before reset are\n26\t  forgotten.\n27\t- A state value that is not one of the five legal one-hot codes transitions to `IDLE` on the next clock.\n28\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f264a491-1a5b-45b0-b4db-6897cccd5411","uuid":"4d05afa4-b0f0-4b3c-ac59-9fe8bfa1a85e","timestamp":"2026-10-19T23:50:05.024Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\Users\\abhig\\Documents\\New folder\\assay\\runs\\v2__seq_detector__t2\\workspace\\spec.md","content":"# seq_detector\n\nA Moore finite-state machine that detects the serial bit pattern `1011` on `din`, with overlapping\nmatches allowed. The state register must be **one-hot** encoded.\n\n## Ports\n| Port  | Dir | Width | Description |\n|-------|-----|-------|-------------|\n| clk   | in  | 1     | Rising-edge clock |\n| rst_n | in  | 1     | Asynchronous active-low reset |\n| din   | in  | 1     | Serial input, sampled on each rising edge of `clk` |\n| found | out | 1     | Registered detection flag |\n\n## Behavior\n- One bit of `din` is sampled at every rising edge of `clk` (there is no enable).\n- The FSM tracks the longest suffix of the bits sampled so far (since reset) that is a prefix of\n  `1011`. States (one-hot): `IDLE` (no useful prefix), `S1` (\"1\"), `S10` (\"10\"), `S101` (\"101\"),\n  `S1011` (full match).\n- Matches overlap: the trailing bits of one match may begin the next. For example the input\n  stream `1011011` produces two matches, and `10101011` produces a match at its last bit\n  (the `101` inside `1010` must not be thrown away).\n- `found` is a flip-flop. After the rising edge at which the final `1` of a `1011` pattern is\n  sampled, `found` is 1 for that clock cycle (i.e. `found` is 1 exactly while the FSM is in\n  `S1011`); otherwise it is 0.\n- While `rst_n` is low the FSM is in `IDLE` and `found` is 0. Bits sampled before reset are\n  forgotten.\n- A state value that is not one of the five legal one-hot codes transitions to `IDLE` on the next clock.\n","numLines":28,"startLine":1,"totalLines":28}}}
